    "stem": "Firoz uses the odd-number square identity $(n-1)^{2}+(2n-1)=n^{2}$. When $2n-1$ is itself a square $m^{2}$, the triple $(m,\\,n-1,\\,n)$ is a Baudh\\={a}yana triple. Here $2n-1=49$.\n\nWhich claim is correct?",
    "item_type": "mcq",
    "options": {
      "A": "The case gives the triple $(7,\\,24,\\,25)$.",
      "B": "The case gives the triple $(49,\\,24,\\,25)$.",
      "C": "The case gives the triple $(7,\\,25,\\,24)$.",
      "D": "The same case still gives a Baudh\\={a}yana triple when $2n-1$ is an odd number that is not a square."
    },
